  "question": {
    "text": "An ideal gas expands isothermally from volume <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;and then it is adiabatically compressed back to its original volume <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>.&nbsp;The initial and final pressures of the gas are&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;respectively and the net&nbsp;work done by the gas is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>W</mi></math>,&nbsp;then",
    "image": null
  },
